course: dictionary
lesson: 12012
title: Chemically: a word from science
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Chemically» is an adverb. It tells you how something is done or changed, and it comes from «chemical», a word about chemistry, the study of what things are made of and how they change. Adverbs often end in -ly, so look for the root word inside: chemical.
explain: «The wood was chemically treated so that rain could not rot it.» The clue is treated: someone used chemicals to change the wood. You do not need to know which chemicals. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The wood was treated with chemicals" still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: Science gives one more idea. In a chemical change, a new substance is made, like when iron rusts. Melting ice is different: it is a physical change, because the water is still water. «The iron changed chemically when it rusted in the rain.» The iron became a new substance.
fact: Chemically means in a way that involves chemistry or chemicals.
fact: «Chemically» can also mean at the level of atoms and molecules, as in «Chemically, water is H2O.»
fact: Chemically identical means made of exactly the same substances.
fact: Chemically different means made of different substances, even if they look alike.
fact: Burning wood and rusting iron change chemically, but melting ice does not.
exercise: «The wood was chemically treated so that rain could not rot it.» What does chemically mean here? | In a loud and noisy way | In a slow way, like a snail | *In a way that uses chemicals | In a happy way
exercise: When iron rusts, it changes chemically into a new substance. Which one is a chemical change? | Ice melting into water | A balloon being blown up | *Iron turning into rust
exercise: «Chemically, water is H2O.» What does this sentence tell you? | Where water comes from | How water tastes | *What water is made of, at the level of atoms and molecules | How cold water is
test: «The two cups of water looked the same, and they were chemically identical.» What does chemically identical mean? | Both hot | Both empty | Made of different substances that look alike | *Made of exactly the same substances
test: «Burning a log is a chemical change, but melting ice is not.» Which one is a chemical change? | Stretching a rubber band | *Burning a log | Melting ice
exam: «The cleaner is chemically strong, so wear gloves.» What does chemically tell you? | *It is made with strong chemicals | It is very quiet | It smells of flowers
